an array member's alignment is its ELEMENT's, not its total size.

MEASURED, and the numbers are the whole argument:

    struct { char a[3]; }    micro-c 4    C says 3
    struct { char a[8]; }    micro-c 8    C says 8     (agrees by luck)
    struct { char a[10]; }   micro-c 16   C says 10
    ArHdr                    micro-c 64   C says 60

largest_member_size drives the struct's own alignment, and the struct's SIZE
is rounded up to it at the bottom of create_struct. Counting `char a[16]` as a
sixteen-byte member rounded that to eight, so a struct of nothing but char
arrays came out a multiple of eight bytes long. An array is aligned to its
ELEMENT, and this file already records both numbers: `i->size` is
count * element, `i->type->size` is the element.

WHAT IT BROKE, WHICH IS NOT A ROUNDING ERROR. ArHdr is tcctools.c's `ar`
member header -- a BYTE-EXACT file format:

    typedef struct {
        char ar_name[16]; char ar_date[12]; char ar_uid[6];
        char ar_gid[6];   char ar_mode[8];  char ar_size[10];
        char ar_fmag[2];
    } ArHdr;                                        tcctools.c:36

tcc writes it with `fwrite(&arhdr, sizeof(arhdr), 1, fh)`, so mc-tcc wrote
SIXTY-FOUR bytes where sixty belong and four stray bytes landed exactly where
the archive's symbol table starts. The archive mc-tcc produced, mc-tcc then
rejected:

    mc-tcc -ar libh.a h1.o     ->  1190 bytes
    mc-tcc ... libh.a          ->  libh.a: error: invalid archive

`tcc -ar` is on stage 4's critical path and WHAT-STAGE-4-NEEDS says why it
cannot be stubbed: binutils has to be built by tcc, and building it means
creating libbfd.a and libiberty.a before any `ar` exists.

FOUND BY COMPARING AGAINST THE CONTROL, byte for byte. The gcc-built tcc's
archive and mc-tcc's differ in the symbol-table member:

    control   00000001 | 00000054 | "helper\0" \0
    mc-tcc    00000000 00000001 | 00000058 | "help"        -- four bytes long

and hofs, printed from inside mc-tcc, read 88 where 8 + 60 + 7 + 8 = 84 is
right. Four bytes, in one place, from one sizeof.

NON-ARRAY MEMBERS ARE UNTOUCHED: for those, `i->size` and `i->type->size` are
the same number, so the rule only moves what it is about. The rounding itself
stays -- it was added because a struct whose size is not a multiple of its
alignment puts every array element after the first at a misaligned address,
and the comment above it records that. What changes is the input.

MEASURED, LOCALLY, EITHER SIDE:

    difftest amd64        98 pass, 0 fail, 2 known gaps      unchanged
    difftest aarch64     100 pass, 0 fail, 2 known gaps      unchanged
    stage 2 corpus       419 of 426                          unchanged

    archive              1190 -> 1182 bytes, the control's size exactly
    symbol table         byte-identical to the control's

NOT YET FIXED, AND IT IS A DIFFERENT LAYER. The archive is still rejected: the
object member's own sixty-byte header is lost when tcc copies its temp file
back --

    fseek(fo, 0, SEEK_END); fsize = ftell(fo);
    fseek(fo, 0, SEEK_SET); fread(buf, fsize, 1, fo);
    fwrite(buf, fsize, 1, fh);                      tcctools.c:317

-- and M2libc's ftell returns `stream->bufpos`, a position within the buffer
rather than in the file, with `/* No ftell for you */` where the real answer
belongs. That is a runtime gap of the same kind as realpath's ownership bug,
not a compiler defect, and it wants its own change.

